---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import "../styles/global.css";

const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Restaurant",
  name: "Locanda Verde",
  servesCuisine: ["Italian"],
  priceRange: "$$$",
  telephone: "+1-212-925-3797",
  url: "https://www.thegreenwichhotel.com",
  address: {
    "@type": "PostalAddress",
    streetAddress: "377 Greenwich St",
    addressLocality: "New York",
    addressRegion: "NY",
    postalCode: "10013",
    addressCountry: "US",
  },
  description:
    "Andrew Carmellini's Italian restaurant inside The Greenwich Hotel in Tribeca — rustic cooking, serious pasta, and a room that buzzes nightly.",
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Locanda Verde Menu Guide — Prices & What to Order NYC"
  description="Independent guide to Locanda Verde at The Greenwich Hotel: antipasti, pasta and secondi with reported prices from the restaurant's 2026 menu, our picks, and visiting tips."
  path="/locanda-verde-menu/"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  ogImage="/images/locanda-verde.webp"
>
  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6">
    <img
      src="/images/locanda-verde.webp"
      alt="Illustration of a warm rustic Italian restaurant dining room with an open kitchen — an artist's interpretation, not the actual Locanda Verde"
      class="w-full h-64 sm:h-96 object-cover rounded-2xl mt-8"
      fetchpriority="high"
    />

    <p class="mt-8 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-gold-dark">
      Menu Guide · Tribeca · The Greenwich Hotel
    </p>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl sm:text-5xl font-bold mt-2 leading-tight">
      Locanda Verde — Menu Guide
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-ink-soft leading-relaxed">
      On the ground floor of The Greenwich Hotel — the Tribeca hotel co-founded by Robert De
      Niro — chef Andrew Carmellini runs one of downtown's most reliable Italian dining rooms.
      The cooking is rustic but precise: a proper pasta program, a wood-fired grill, and a room
      that hums every night of the week. Below is our independent walkthrough of the menu with
      reported prices from the restaurant's published 2026 menu, plus what we'd order. Everything
      is written in our own words; prices are reported figures, not a copy of the menu.
    </p>

    <!-- At a glance -->
    <div class="mt-8 rounded-2xl border border-line bg-white p-6 sm:p-8">
      <h2 class="!mt-0 font-display text-xl font-bold">At a glance</h2>
      <dl class="mt-4 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2 text-sm">
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Location</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">377 Greenwich St, New York, NY 10013 (inside The Greenwich Hotel, Tribeca)</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Chef</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">Andrew Carmellini</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Cuisine</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">Rustic Italian — antipasti, handmade pasta, wood-fired secondi</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Price level</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">Moderate-upscale — pasta reported $30–39, secondi $42–82</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Reservations</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">Strongly recommended; the room fills nightly</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Phone</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">+1 (212) 925-3797</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-guide">
      <h2>Ostini &amp; Antipasti</h2>
      <p>
        Start at the top of the menu. The ostini (little toasts, reported <strong>$18–25</strong>)
        are the move with a first drink, and the antipasti run <strong>$15–35</strong>. This is
        where the kitchen shows its range — from a simple ricotta to a wagyu tartare with black truffle.
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-line my-6 not-prose">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-parchment text-left">
              <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Dish</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-right">Reported price</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-line bg-white">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Steak tartare Piedmontese — wagyu, hazelnut, black truffle</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right whitespace-nowrap">$35</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Big eye tuna with caperberry tonnato</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right whitespace-nowrap">$30</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Fritto misto with lemon</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right whitespace-nowrap">$28</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Insalata D'Andrea — arugula, apple, almond, asiago</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right whitespace-nowrap">$26</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">The Green Caesar with anchovy crouton crumble</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right whitespace-nowrap">$25</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Carpaccio of beets with gorgonzola, clementine, pistachio</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right whitespace-nowrap">$24</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Gabagool &amp; grana</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right whitespace-nowrap">$22</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Focaccia Napoletana — tomato, fior di latte, oregano</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right whitespace-nowrap">$15</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Lamb meatball sliders with caprino and cucumber pickles</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right whitespace-nowrap">$16</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>Primi</h2>
      <p>
        The pasta course is the heart of the restaurant, reported at <strong>$30–39</strong>. Shapes
        change with the seasons, but the structure holds: a filled pasta, a seafood pasta, a couple of
        classics, and the grandmother's ravioli that regulars swear by.
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-line my-6 not-prose">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-parchment text-left">
              <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Pasta</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-right">Reported price</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-line bg-white">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Black shells frutti di mare — rock shrimp, scallops, clams</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right whitespace-nowrap">$39</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Pumpkin ravioli with sage, amaretti, mostarda</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right whitespace-nowrap">$35</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Pappardelle — lamb bolognese, ricotta and mint</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right whitespace-nowrap">$35</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">My Grandmother's ravioli</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right whitespace-nowrap">$34</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Bucatini all'amatriciana</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right whitespace-nowrap">$33</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Spaghetti con acciughe — anchovy butter, savory breadcrumbs</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right whitespace-nowrap">$30</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Our take: the pappardelle is the order if you only eat one pasta — the lamb bolognese with
        mint is the dish that built this restaurant's reputation.
      </p>

      <h2>Secondi</h2>
      <p>
        Mains come off the wood-fired grill, reported from <strong>$42 to $82</strong>. The
        fire-roasted garlic chicken is the signature — order the whole bird for the table if there
        are three or more of you.
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-line my-6 not-prose">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-parchment text-left">
              <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Main</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-right">Reported price</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-line bg-white">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Fire-roasted garlic chicken — whole bird</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right whitespace-nowrap">$82</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Prime NY strip with Sicilian trapanese, salsa verde</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right whitespace-nowrap">$72</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Atlantic halibut alla ghiotta</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right whitespace-nowrap">$52</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Pork chop Lorenzo with pepper sauce and polenta</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right whitespace-nowrap">$50</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Branzino arrabbiata with salsa gialla, fennel relish</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right whitespace-nowrap">$45</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Fire-roasted garlic chicken — half bird</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right whitespace-nowrap">$42</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Contorni (sides) run <strong>$17–18</strong> — the roasted brussels sprouts with guanciale
        and the frico potatoes are the ones worth the supplement.
      </p>

      <h2>What we'd order</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>To start:</strong> the lamb meatball sliders — small, rich, and gone in four bites.</li>
        <li><strong>The pasta:</strong> pappardelle with lamb bolognese. Non-negotiable.</li>
        <li><strong>The main:</strong> the fire-roasted garlic chicken (whole bird for the table) — it's the dish the room is known for.</li>
        <li><strong>The sleeper:</strong> black shells frutti di mare if you lean seafood — it's the priciest pasta and earns it.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Planning your visit</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Book ahead.</strong> Locanda Verde is a neighborhood institution that happens to be in a hotel — prime-time tables go fast.</li>
        <li><strong>It's the best value on this list.</strong> Full pasta-and-main dinners here run meaningfully less than the Midtown hotel rooms — a good first stop on a hotel-restaurant tour.</li>
        <li><strong>Sit where the energy is.</strong> The room runs loud and happy at peak hours; ask for the main dining room over a corner table if you want the buzz.</li>
        <li><strong>Tribeca parking is rough</strong> — subway or car service beats circling for a spot.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- Price disclaimer -->
    <div class="mt-10 rounded-xl border border-gold/40 bg-gold/10 p-5 text-sm text-ink-soft leading-relaxed">
      <p class="font-semibold text-ink mb-1">About these prices</p>
      <p>
        Prices above are <strong>reported from the restaurant's published early-2026 menu</strong>.
        Menus change seasonally and prices move — always verify current prices with the restaurant
        before visiting.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-8 text-sm text-ink-soft">
      <a href="/" class="text-gold-dark font-semibold hover:underline">← Back to all guides</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
